+\begin{lemma}[Simultaneous finite profiles]\label{lem:weak-finite-profiles}
+Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:bl-HJ}, let $I$ be finite,
+let $X$ be a set, and let $\tau_i\colon\AM^n_1\to X$ for $i\in I$.
+For every finite colouring $\chi$ of $X$, there is one $h\in\AM^n_2$
+such that
+$$
+ \chi(\tau_i(h\circ e))
+ =\chi(\tau_i(h\restriction_{T({\leq}n)}))
+ \qquad(i\in I,\ e\in\Gamma_n).
+$$
+\end{lemma}
+\begingroup\renewcommand{\leancommit}{70ae3b4e71de18d16dae2276a389574fc05da7d9}
+\leaninterface{SuccessorTree/Block/SeededPigeonhole.lean}{finiteProfilePigeonhole}
+\endgroup
+\begin{proof}
+Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:bl-HJ} to the finite tuple colouring
+$$
+ f\longmapsto (\chi(\tau_i(f)))_{i\in I}.
+$$
+Equality of the tuples gives all the asserted equalities for the same
+$h$. In particular, choosing a separate realisation for each profile
+is neither needed nor used.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{lemma}[A covered family]\label{lem:weak-covered-family}
+With the same data, suppose that to every $h\in\AM^n_2$ there are
+assigned a set $\Phi(h)\subseteq X$ and a point $a(h)\in X$ such that
+$$
+ \Phi(h)\subseteq
+ \bigcup_{i\in I}\left(
+ \{\tau_i(h\restriction_{T({\leq}n)})\}
+ \cup\{\tau_i(h\circ e):e\in\Gamma_n\}\right),
+$$
+and $\tau_i(h\restriction_{T({\leq}n)})=a(h)$ for all $i\in I$.
+For every finite colouring $\chi$ of $X$, some $h$ satisfies
+$\chi(t)=\chi(a(h))$ for every $t\in\Phi(h)$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begingroup\renewcommand{\leancommit}{70ae3b4e71de18d16dae2276a389574fc05da7d9}
+\leaninterface{SuccessorTree/Block/SeededPigeonhole.lean}{coveredFrontPigeonhole}
+\endgroup
+\begin{proof}
+Choose $h$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:weak-finite-profiles}. Represent each
+$t\in\Phi(h)$ using the displayed inclusion. Its colour equals the
+colour of the corresponding profile at the common base, hence equals
+$\chi(a(h))$.
+\end{proof}
+The common-base condition cannot be dropped merely because the tuple
+colouring is finite: it makes each profile homogeneous separately,
+not necessarily in the same colour. Without that condition, the same
+proof bounds the number of colours by $|I|$ when the inclusion holds.
+
+These lemmas are conditional tools, not new axioms. For fixed finite
+$I$ and $\Gamma_n$, the represented family is finite. Thus this is a
+\emph{bounded-stage} lemma, not a proof of (A4) for an unbounded
+front. A fat-tree application must exhaust all one-block extensions
+through finite boundary stages, prove a compatible common base (or
+further identify its colours), and preserve earlier decisions under
+later aligned reductions. One fusion must realise the whole sequence.
+Nonemptiness of the selected family must also be proved; it is not a
+consequence of the inclusion alone. The local line condition does not
+supply these facts. For example, the singleton alphabet can have every
+coded point and every line value equal to $0$ in $\{0,1\}$, while the
+family $\{0,1\}$ is not monochromatic for the identity colouring.
+This finite example is only a counterexample to an abstract coverage
+inference; the genuine successor-monoid obstruction is
+Proposition~\ref{prop:bl-parity}.
+
